# .github/allowed_signers -- RFC-005 slice 30: the committed trust root
# the release workflow's `release-publish` job verifies a release tag's
# SSH signature against (`git -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=.github/
# allowed_signers tag -v <tag>`), rather than querying GitHub's own
# signing-key API. Two deliberate reasons, both recorded here rather than
# left implicit:
#
#   1. No API dependency: GitHub's REST API has no endpoint that returns
#      "the SSH public keys this account has registered for signing" --
#      only the web UI exposes that, and the `verification` field GitHub
#      attaches to a tag/commit object reflects GitHub's OWN opinion of
#      the signature, checked against whatever it has on file at read
#      time. Verifying with git itself against a file this repository
#      controls needs no GitHub-side state at all.
#   2. A reviewable trust root IN-REPO, matching SECURITY.md's "Trust
#      root / security posture" section: every reader can see exactly
#      which key(s) this project trusts to sign a release tag, in a
#      diff-reviewable commit, rather than "whatever GitHub currently has
#      on file for this account" (which could include a key added for an
#      unrelated purpose, or be silently rotated with no repo-side
#      record of the change).
#
# Format: git's own ssh "allowed signers" file
# (https://man.openbsd.org/ssh-keygen#ALLOWED_SIGNERS), one principal per
# line -- `<principal> <key-type> <base64-key>`. The principal is matched
# against the tag/commit's own committer/tagger email by `git tag -v`/
# `git verify-commit`; it does not need to be a real, reachable address.
#
# corey@leavitt.dev is the tagger identity every sello release tag has
# used to date (verified against v0.1.0 through v0.3.0's own tag
# objects); the key below is Corey's SSH signing key
# (~/.ssh/id_ed25519.github.pub on the maintainer's own machine, the same
# key `git config user.signingkey` already points at locally --
# `gpg.format=ssh`, `tag.gpgsign=true`). A real, live verification against
# this exact file (`git -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=.github/
# allowed_signers tag -v v0.3.0`) was run before this file was committed:
# "Good git signature for corey@leavitt.dev".
#
# Rotation ritual: if the signing key ever changes, add a NEW line here
# (never delete the old one outright without cause -- a still-valid
# historical tag signed under the old key must keep verifying), then
# start signing new tags with the new key. This file is covered by
# scripts/policy-lint.sh only insofar as release.yml itself is a workflow
# file (SHA-pinned actions, permissions, etc.) -- this file's own content
# is reviewed by ordinary commit review, the same trust model as every
# other committed pin file in this repo (gates.txt, image-pins.txt).
